181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (In populated urban areas such as Boston, Seattle, and Washington DC, increased tourism activity contributes to resident service burden, which is the added strain placed on city services and infrastructure by visitor demand. This burden often appears through higher levels of noise, overcrowding in public spaces, sanitation concerns, and transportation congestion. These effects are commonly described in tourism research as tourism spillovers, where visitor activity creates unintended impacts on local residents and city services.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Tourism impacts are often assumed to increase in a simple, linear way as tourism activity grows. It varies widely across cities due to differences in infrastructure capacity, tourism management, and local conditions. As a result, similar increases in tourism activity may lead to very different levels of resident burden in different cities.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (This project examines whether tourism activity is associated with increases in resident service burden and whether this relationship differs across cities. By combining Google Trends data as a measure of tourism activity with 311 service request data as an indicator of resident burden, the analysis explores how tourism pressure interacts with urban quality of life over time.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
