228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (This project combines two publicly available datasets to examine the relationship between tourism pressure and resident service burden across major U.S. cities.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (Using Google Trends, a tool that analyzes the popularity of top search queries, tourism activity was measured. To do this, tourism-related keywords were selected in order to represent interest in visiting, staying in, or engaging in activities within each city. Google Trends reports monthly search interest on a normalized scale from 0-100, where 100 represents peak relative interest for that city during the selected time range. These values were used as a proxy for tourism demand over time.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (Using city 311 service request data, resident service burden was measured. This data was obtained from city open-data portals and datasets record non-emergency complaints submitted by residents about every day concerns relating to quality-of-life, such as noise, sanitation, congestion, and limited public space. For consistency across cities, only the complaint type and date fields were retained.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 141 0 R
/T (2. Data Preparation)
/E (2. Data Preparation)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (All datasets were standardized to a monthly, city-level time scale to allow for direct comparison between tourism activities and resident complaints.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (311 service request records were processed and converted into a standard date format. Each complaint was labeled as either tourism-related or non-tourism-related using a predefined keyword-based filtering approach. Keywords were selected to capture common tourism spillover effects, such as noise, nightlife activity, crowding, transportation congestion, and short-term visitor accommodations. The complete list of keywords used for classification is included in Appendix B.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (For each city and month, complaint data was aggregated in order to produce monthly totals. Google Trends data was downloaded at a monthly frequency and aligned to the same time range as the 311 data. Months with missing or incomplete data in either dataset were removed to ensure valid comparisons.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
