262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (Overall, the results showed that tourism activity and tourism-related complaint share had a positive association, which implies that periods of higher tourism demand coincided with high proportions of resident complaints of to tourism spillovers. However, the strength and consistency of this relationship varied by city, which suggests that cities experience and manage tourism pressure differently.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (In some cities, increased tourism intensity was associated with a gradual increase in complaints, which showed that resident burden rose proportionally alongside tourism demand. In other cities, the complaint shares increased more drastically during high-tourism periods, which suggests the presence of saturation effects, where the additional tourism demand results in a disproportionately higher resident service strain.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
