/P 150 0 R
/E (This TSA Data Science and Analytics project explored the relationship between tourism activity and resident service burden, based on how they are associated and whether this relationship differs across cities. By combining Google Trends tourism data with resident-filed 311 service requests, the analysis offers insight into how changes in tourism demand relate to urban quality-of-life impacts.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (The results show that higher tourism activity is generally linked to increases in tourism-related resident complaints. However, this relationship varies by city, indicating that tourism pressure does not affect all urban areas in the same way. Some cities appear better able to accommodate tourism demand with relatively smaller increases in resident complaints, while others experience sharper rises during periods of high tourism activity.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (Seasonal patterns and city-specific differences suggest that local conditions such as infrastructure capacity, service availability, and tourism management practices play an important role in shaping resident experiences. Cities with stronger planning and public service capacity may be more resilient to increased tourism, while cities facing rapid tourism growth may experience greater strain.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (Overall, these findings highlight that tourism growth can contribute to resident service burden, but the scale of its impact depends heavily on local context. This underscores the importance of using city-specific data to inform tourism management strategies rather than relying on uniform approaches across different urban environments.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
